#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167938} :activity/id #uuid "aee81a52-a15f-57d8-b2cd-df2429271786" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050989 :topic/id #uuid "7742668d-ad15-5c25-bc82-4ea5b4fd800d" :topic/math-academy-id 3198} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167938 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168045 :tutorial/content "The **Fourier convergence theorem** describes how a Fourier series behaves, especially at points where the original function is not continuous.\n\nA Fourier series is built from smooth sine and cosine waves, so it cannot “jump” instantly the way a function with a jump discontinuity can. Instead, the series *converges* to an average value at these jumps.\n\nMore precisely, let $f(x)$ be a $2L$-periodic piecewise smooth function, and let its Fourier series be\n\n$$\nS(x)=\\dfrac{a_0}{2}+\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}\\left(\na_n\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi}{L}x\\right)+b_n\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi}{L}x\\right)\n\\right).\n$$\n\nThen the Fourier series converges for every $x\\in\\mathbb{R}$. The value it converges to depends on whether $f$ is continuous at $x$.\n\n- If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x,$ the series converges to the function value:\n\n$$\nS(x)=f(x)\n$$\n\n- If $f(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x,$ the series converges to the midpoint of the one-sided limits:\n\n$$\nS(x)=\\dfrac{f^-(x)+f^+(x)}{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $f^-(x)$ and $f^+(x)$ are the left- and right-sided limits of $f$ at $x,$ respectively.\n\nLet's see a concrete example." :tutorial/id #uuid "7d62d7d1-1939-536d-8fef-b02c4d023b32" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14717 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "0daa028f-4325-5685-88f2-3cbbcf622af2" :step/math-academy-id 598800 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167957}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167939 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166960 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166575 :question/id #uuid "7e478876-8258-54a8-9dd7-81978b7cf9af" :question/math-academy-id "e-21865"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5f9dcbb0-d943-5340-916e-d789b6ab03bc" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048443} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049995}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167292} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167293} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317392} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317393} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317394} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317395} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317396} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317397} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375031} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375034} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375055}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Value to What a Fourier Series Converges"} :step/id #uuid "31151663-c118-573c-a32e-a71852a7ab4b" :step/math-academy-id 598801 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167940}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167940 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168046 :tutorial/content "A powerful application of Fourier series is *evaluating infinite sums*. The strategy is to find a known Fourier series that contains the sum we want, and then choose a convenient value of $x$ to plug in.\n\nFor example, consider the function $F(x)=x^2$ defined on $x \\in [-\\pi,\\pi).$ The Fourier series of the $2\\pi$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x) \\sim \\dfrac{\\pi^2}{3} + 4\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{(-1)^n}{n^2} \\cos(nx).\n$$\n\nLet's use this to find the value of the infinite sum $\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{(-1)^n}{n^2}.$ \n\nNow, notice that at $x=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\cos\\left(n\\cdot0\\right) = \\cos\\left(0\\right) = 1.\n$$\n\nBy the *pointwise convergence theorem*, since $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=0,$ the corresponding Fourier series converges to \n\n$$\nf\\left(0\\right) = 0.\n$$\n\n \n\nHence, we have the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0) & =\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{3}+4{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}(n\\cdot 0) \\\\ 0 & =\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{3}+4{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}}\\cdot 1 \\\\ -\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{3} & =4{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{12} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{(-1)^n}{n^2} = -\\dfrac{\\pi^2}{12}.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "8db745e6-4117-50ca-a3ea-393692db7e84" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14721 :tutorial/title "Using Fourier Series For Finding Infinite Sums"} :step/id #uuid "4bdf108a-d330-5888-ae50-49c4e0f66ede" :step/math-academy-id 598804 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167941}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167941 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166961 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166576 :question/id #uuid "0e966ca9-eab2-5443-9581-9b7cb8ecd4b2" :question/math-academy-id "e-21866"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b3cb7656-1ce6-5b6a-9e88-5ba24a943c3f" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167294} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167295} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317399} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317401} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317402} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375037} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402284} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402302} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402320} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402338} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420495}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Value of an Infinite Sum Using Fourier Series"} :step/id #uuid "42cd3b2e-2abc-5095-b2fe-87d2a522be3b" :step/math-academy-id 598805 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167942}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167942 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168047 :tutorial/content "Even though the Fourier series converges to the correct value at each point (using the midpoint rule at jumps), the *graphs of the partial sums* can behave strangely near a jump discontinuity.\n\nSuppose $f(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x=x_0$. Then:\n\n- The Fourier series converges at the jump to the midpoint:\n\n$$\nS(x_0)=\\dfrac{f^-(x_0)+f^+(x_0)}{2}.\n$$\n\n- However, for large $N$, the partial sum\n\n$$\nS_N(x)=\\dfrac{a_0}{2}+\\sum_{n=1}^{N}\\left(\na_n\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi}{L}x\\right)+b_n\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi}{L}x\\right)\n\\right)\n$$\n\ntypically *overshoots* above the higher side of the jump and *undershoots* below the lower side.\n\nAs $N$ increases, the oscillations get squeezed into a smaller neighborhood around $x_0$, but the size of the overshoot does *not* go to $0$. This persistent overshoot near jump discontinuities is called the **Gibbs phenomenon**.\n\nFor example, for the Fourier series of the $2\\pi$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of the function $F(x)=x,$ defined on $[-\\pi,\\pi),$ the phenomenon can be observed at each point of discontinuity on the graph below.\n\n![](images/55/55a772d1f18a80a16772e6a898917a3ad863ee33a3c5b482d9201b7d22c91089.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "c33817ab-d849-5e36-8059-f0cb3e6146cd" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14722 :tutorial/title "Gibbs Phenomenon"} :step/id #uuid "bba9f02f-db71-5d7b-8afd-c5b08062d34c" :step/math-academy-id 598808} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167950 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168048 :tutorial/content "As we saw, the partial sums $S_N(x)$ appear to converge to the function $f(x)$ as $N\\to\\infty$. The *Fourier convergence theorem* specifies the exact value to which the series converges.\n\n- At any point $x_0$ where $f(x)$ is *continuous*, the Fourier series converges to the function value:\n\n$$\nS(x_0)=f(x_0)\n$$\n\n- At any point $x_0$ where $f(x)$ has a *jump discontinuity*, the Fourier series converges to the midpoint of the jump (the average of the one-sided limits):\n\n$$\nS(x_0)=\\dfrac{f^-(x_0)+f^+(x_0)}{2}\n$$\n\n![](images/52/5261e83960334e21396e3c7e3e2c0af82154dba29f99a338f22d5ef615d1af80.png)\n\nFor our sawtooth function above, the discontinuities occur at $x=(2k+1)\\pi$. At $x=\\pi$, for example, the series converges to:\n\n$$\nS(\\pi)=\\dfrac{f^-(\\pi)+f^+(\\pi)}{2} = \\dfrac{\\pi + (-\\pi)}{2} = 0\n$$\n\nThe graph of the limit function $S(x)$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/c5/c5731fc49b0bca19ae60b9a5bc14eae5ee64f8ff36ebae85dcf2ffe0a4270708.png)\n\n**Note:** The limit function $S(x)$ is the same as $f(x)$ except at the jump discontinuities. If $f(x)$ were continuous everywhere, then its Fourier series would converge to $f(x)$ at every point, and we could write $f(x)=S(x)$ instead of $f(x)\\sim S(x)$." :tutorial/id #uuid "73760171-1fe4-5fe1-ae35-b5efeeb39cdc" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14761 :tutorial/title "An Example of Convergence"} :step/id #uuid "18f33be1-5014-56eb-a799-174a9420a064" :step/math-academy-id 601089 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167939}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167957 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168054 :tutorial/content "Consider the function $F(x)=x$ defined on $[-\\pi,\\pi).$ The Fourier series of its $2\\pi$-periodic extension $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x) \\sim S(x) = \\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty} \\dfrac{2(-1)^{n+1}}{n}\\sin\\left(nx\\right).\n$$\n\nThe graph of the function $y=f(x)$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/52/5261e83960334e21396e3c7e3e2c0af82154dba29f99a338f22d5ef615d1af80.png)\n\nWe can approximate the function $f(x)$ by summing a finite number of terms from its Fourier series. These approximations are called *partial sums*:\n\n$$\nS_N(x)=\\sum_{n=1}^{N}\\dfrac{2(-1)^{n+1}}{n}\\sin\\left(nx\\right)\n$$\n\nAs $N$ increases, the partial sums match the graph of $f(x)$ more closely.\n\n![](images/4c/4c3020fcc3aa06bec65384bb3ba852b289fd1d705e554d39cc3be9b41db6623d.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "dcb1351c-d635-5660-9fec-0f4c5c5f9018" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14872 :tutorial/title "An Example of Approximation"} :step/id #uuid "48047656-abbd-570b-8b4c-235ee9655e83" :step/math-academy-id 607545 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167950}}] :activity/title "Convergence of Fourier Series" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166993}]}
